00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:08.300
has stolen that our TL made
that literally just crops

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.295
a spreadsheet to the
cells that you're using.

00:17:10.295 --> 00:17:13.109
So you don't have
1,000 rows by accident.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:14.213
Love that thing.

00:17:14.213 --> 00:17:15.630
But the other thing
is Apps Script

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:16.724
can still solve problems.

00:17:16.724 --> 00:17:18.349
Maybe you don't have
access to the data

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:22.050
set that you need to include in
your analysis for some reason.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:23.720
It's some other team,
but it's relevant

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.579
to your entire initiative.

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:27.770
But if you get a monthly
update email from them,

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:30.020
you could write an Apps
Script to look for that email,

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:32.000
copy the information
over, and then include it

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.190
in your work
automatically without ever

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:37.070
having to read the email because
it could still be filtered.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:38.357
We all love our filters.

00:17:38.357 --> 00:17:40.190
And if you don't know
how your filters work,

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:45.045
you should talk to me in about
three minutes and 11, 10, 9

00:17:45.045 --> 00:17:45.545
seconds.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:49.893
So it basically never ends.

00:17:49.893 --> 00:17:52.310
And then there's a lot more
things that actually can help.

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:54.765
Like, if you haven't started
with Apps Script yet,

00:17:54.765 --> 00:17:56.390
and you're terrified
of JavaScript that

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:59.630
dates back to the 1990s or
something like that, that's OK,

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:00.200
too.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:02.990
Because last year, we
released the macro recorder

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:04.100
for Google Sheets.

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:05.750
And what the macro
recorder does is

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:08.480
it records every click that
you make inside of your sheet

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.353
so that you can color
everything once, make a new tab,

00:18:11.353 --> 00:18:12.770
and then run it
again and not have

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:15.050
to go through all
of those 78 clicks

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:16.830
every single time
you have a new tab.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:17.990
And this is a great thing.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:20.240
But what's really cool is
you can edit the macros that

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:21.122
were made for you.

00:18:21.122 --> 00:18:22.580
And if you do that,
you'll actually

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:25.880
get a fully written Apps Script,
a very tedious robotically

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:26.810
written code.

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:28.710
But it can show you
how things work.

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:30.980
So if you're having some
trouble with A1 notation,

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:33.560
record what you're trying to
do as a macro, look at it,

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:36.380
and then steal
from the computer.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:36.880
Yeah.

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:39.047
This is how engineering
works, and everyone's always

00:18:39.047 --> 00:18:39.550
intimidated.

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:41.240
You're like, yeah, all we
do is go to Stack Overflow

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:42.890
and then steal things
that are already written.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:44.300
ALICIA WILLIAMS: You
can even macro record

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:46.050
refreshing the data
from the data connector.

